    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A permit to work protects the riskiest jobs on a site. Hot work. Confined space. Electrical isolation. Work at height. A competent person authorises the task and the controls. When it goes wrong, the permit was usually a formality. It was signed in a hurry, or written up after the job. A permit to work app should make the checks happen in order, and prove they did.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A permit controls high-risk tasks: hot work, confined space, electrical isolation and work at height.</li><li>Confined space entry sits under the Confined Spaces Regulations 1997. Work at height sits under the Work at Height Regulations 2005. Both sit under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974.</li><li>The permit is the proof the controls were checked. It is not the control.</li><li>Paper books and spreadsheets are often filled in after the fact.</li><li>Quickler records the permit and its checks as the job is authorised. It does not replace the competent authoriser or the risk assessment.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The point",
      "heading": "What a permit to work is for",
      "html": "<p>A permit names the task, the place, the hazards, the precautions, the people and the time. A competent person authorises it before work starts. It is closed and the area handed back when the work ends.</p><p>The permit is not the control. The isolation, the air test, the fire watch and the authoriser are. The permit proves they were in place, and that the area was made safe after. That proof matters if an HSE inspector or an insurer asks. This is not legal advice. Check the current standards.</p>"
    },
    {
